// The 3rd Coming - Overview chapter

=== What This Guide Is

XCOM fell. XCOM rose. *The 3rd Coming* is the name for the third time the resistance answers the call - a curated XCOM 2: War of the Chosen experience built on the final, stable game build (8270065, February 2022), managed with the community-standard Alternative Mod Launcher, and stocked from the Steam Workshop.

The guide is organized by category. Each category chapter explains what its mods do to the game, and every mod appears as a *mod card*: a clickable Workshop link plus the facts that matter for playing with it - what it depends on, what it changes, and how to configure it.

New to XCOM 2, or back after years? The *How to Play* chapter explains the two layers of the game - the Avenger strategy layer and the tactical missions - the classes, the Chosen, and how to survive your first campaign. The *Vanilla Strategy* chapter then gives concrete, wiki-grounded recommendations for building order, squad composition, and research priorities.

=== Ground Rules

+ *The user supplies every mod.* On 2026-08-12 the user supplied the [WOTC] Core Collection (Steam Workshop, 75 mods) as the baseline; every card in this guide is transcribed from that collection page. The project does not add, validate, filter, or research mods.
+ Each card documents exactly what the user supplied: *name + URL* and the collection's own *description*. A field the user did not supply (*dependencies*, *system / mechanic impact*) stays out of the card.
+ Decisions and open questions live in the project's `STATUS.md`; conflicts and future ideas live outside this PDF.

=== How to Read a Mod Card

Every card carries the same four fields:

+ *Name* - the mod's name; clickable and linked to its Workshop page.
+ *Dependencies* - what the mod needs to work, exactly as the user stated them: other mods, the base game, or War of the Chosen features.
+ *Impact* - the system / mechanic the mod touches and what it changes about how the game plays.
+ *Notes* - anything else that matters, transcribed from the collection page: load-order position, configuration steps, or quirks.

As cards are added, each one states exactly this information and nothing more. Fields the user did not supply stay out of the card.

=== The Categories

+ *Core & Framework* - foundational systems that other mods build on.
+ *Quality of Life* - convenience and automation that reduce busywork.
+ *UI & HUD* - interface, screens, and information display.
+ *Classes & Skills* - soldier classes, abilities, and skill trees.
+ *Enemies & Aliens* - the opposition: behavior, roster, and threat.
+ *Weapons & Gear* - items, equipment, and loadout options.
+ *Cosmetics & Visuals* - appearance of soldiers, environments, and effects.
+ *Audio & Sound* - music, voice, and sound design.

The category list is a draft, open to adjustment by the user.
